# 🌐 Networking Basics — Gateways & Network Boundaries

## 📌 Overview
This module covers how routers function as gateways and how they define boundaries between networks.

---

## 🚪 Router as a Default Gateway

A **default gateway** is the device that allows hosts to communicate with devices on other networks.

### 🔑 Key Idea:
- If a host wants to communicate **outside its local network**, it sends data to the **router (default gateway)**.

### 🧠 Concept:
- Same network → Direct communication  
- Different network → Go through router  

---

## 🌍 Router as a Network Boundary

Routers separate different networks and control traffic between them.

### 🔑 Key Functions:
- Connect multiple networks  
- Forward packets between networks  
- Act as a boundary between IP subnets  

### 🧠 Concept:
- Each network is independent  
- Routers define where one network ends and another begins  

---

## 🔍 Important Observations

When two hosts are on **different networks**:

- ✅ They will have **different IP addresses**
- ✅ They will have **different default gateway addresses**
- ❌ They will NOT share the same MAC address
- ❌ They will NOT share the same IP address

---

## 🧠 Golden Rule

> Same Network → Direct Communication  
> Different Network → Use Router (Default Gateway)

---

## 📊 Example Scenario

| Device | IP Address       | Network        | Default Gateway |
|--------|----------------|---------------|-----------------|
| Host A | 192.168.1.10  | 192.168.1.0/24 | 192.168.1.1     |
| Host B | 192.168.2.10  | 192.168.2.0/24 | 192.168.2.1     |

### 🔎 Explanation:
- Host A and Host B are on **different networks**
- They must communicate **through a router**
- Each network has its **own gateway**

---

## 🚀 Takeaway

Routers are:
- The **exit point** for external communication  
- The **boundary** between networks  

Understanding this is critical for:
- Subnetting  
- Routing  
- Network design  
